    "audit_root_causes": {
      "why_predicted_vs_current_traffic_differ": "In most scenarios, current traffic and time-dependent predicted routing choose identical routes. When downstream congestion is predicted to clear, time-dependent routing selects a direct corridor instead of an indirect detour. Depending on ground-truth incident rollouts, the direct path may either save time or suffer temporary delays if congestion clears slower than expected.",
      "why_uncertainty_aware_behavior_occurs": "When uncertainty variance is uniform across edges, adding a penalty proportional to uncertainty acts as a constant cost shift per hop, preserving shortest paths. When uncertainty is heterogeneous (e.g. volatile incident corridors vs stable arterials), uncertainty-aware routing actively detours around high-variance corridors to minimize tail arrival risk."
    }
  },
